-*-Text-*-

Constructs in output language.

*) PROG

The purpose of a PROG is to allow the simulator to use RETURN.
It will never have a name or any variables to be bound.
For the compiler, it means that the statements are to be executed in
order.

*) PROGN

This means that the rest of the list is made of statements to be
executed in order.

Sometimes there will be (PROGN 'comment-data number).  This is
equivalent to just <number> just as it would be in Lisp.

*) PROGN-VALUE

This is like PROGN but it is regarded as having a "value" which is
returned to the containing construct.  The value is that of the last
expression in the PROGN-VALUE.

*) SAME-STATE

This identifies a list of statements that should all be executed and
can be done in any order, or in any combinations in parallel.
The only kind of jump that can appear is a subroutine call.

*) SAME-STATE-PROG

This identifies a list of statements that can be done sequentially or
in parallel, but if they are done sequentially they must not be
interchanged.  No statement depends on the results of any previous
statement in the SAME-STATE-PROG, but it may depend on things which
will be clobbered by later statements.

A jump other than a subroutine call can appear only as the last
statement.  Normally, we expect the compiler to realize automatically
that any unconditional jump can be done in parallel with the previous
instruction.  So the only jumps which are actually same-stated
explicitly are conditional ones; this is done to declare that the
conditional is not testing anything that was computed by a previous
statement in the same-state-prog.

The compiler should also automatically assume SAME-STATE-PROG between
a construct (SAME-STATE-PROG (POP-ARG ...) (POP-ARG ...) ... (URETURN))
and whatever precedes it.

*) SAME-CYCLE

This identifies a list of statements which MUST be executed in
parallel, because otherwise there would be a timing error.  This is
needed only in communication off the chip.  The things that must be
done in one instruction include all branches of any nested IFs that
appear inside the SAME-CYCLE!

*) SAME-STATE-PROG1

This has the same constraints on parallelism as SAME-STATE-PROG, but
it is used as a condition in an IF.  The first statement is itself
suitable for being a condition, and its value serves as the condition
for the IF.

*) SAME-CYCLE-PROG1

This is like SAME-STATE-PROG1 in its interaction with IF, but its
constraints on parallelism are the same as SAME-CYCLE.

*) SAME-CYCLE-VALUE

This is like SAME-CYCLE but it is regarded as having a "value" which
is returned to the containing construct.  The value is that of the
last expression in the SAME-CYCLE-VALUE.

*) (IF condition if-true if-false)

This stands for conditional execution.  <condition> should be a call
to =?, or a SAME-STATE-PROG1 or SAME-CYCLE-PROG1 which starts with a
call to =?

*) (=? exp1 exp2)

This is a condition which is true if the value of <exp1> equals that
of <exp2>.  All conditions are either of this form or composit and
made out of one or more other conditions.

*) NOT, AND and OR

These take conditions as their arguments and the result is a
condition.

*) (GOTO pc)

This indicates an unconditional jump to a specific place (<pc>).

*) (DISPATCH expression table)

This means jump to an address which is the sum of the value of
<expression> and the address of the dispatch table <table>.
The name <table> is specified in the DEFDISPATCHes which define the
entry points.

*) (WAIT-UNTIL condition)

This means to loop until the condition is true.  It is ok for
SAME-STATE or SAME-CYCLE to cause other things to be done at the same
time as the busy wait (in fact, sometimes we depend on it).  Then they
are done each time the instruction is repeated.

*) (URETURN)

This means jump to a pc popped off the microcode pc stack.

*) (CALL-PC pc)

This means jump to <pc> and push on the microcode call stack the
address of the following microinstruction.

*) (PUSH-ARG (ARG-STACK-READ-ONLY expression) arg-stack)

This means push onto <arg-stack> somethingwhich says that the value of
the corresponding argument is the value of <expression>.  <expression>
is either a use of READ-REG or a use of MAKE-POINTER.

*) (PUSH-ARG (ARG-STACK-READ-WRITE register) arg-stack)

This means push onto <arg-stack> something which says that the value
of the corresponding argument is located in <register>.

*) (POP-ARG arg-stack)

This means that the top entry on <arg-stack> should be discarded.

*) (ASSERT bit)

This means to connect <bit> to a 1 signal.  <bit> is a bus (or
subfield of one) of width one.

*) (PUT-WORD-ON-SBUS exp)

This means to connect the WORD subfield of the sbus to signals
containing the value of <exp>, so that that value is sent off the
chip.

*) (READ-SBUS-DATA)

This refers to a value which is whatever the external world is putting
on the WORD subfield of the sbus.

Interrupts (so to speak)

*) (ENABLE-GOTO-SBUS-ERROR)

If during the instruction in which this occurs the bit (BITVEC ERROR
SBUS) is on, (GOTO SBUS-ERROR) is forced.

*) If the flag ENABLE-CONS-SERVER is set, then (UCALL
CONS-SERVER-ENTRY) can be forced at any time if the bit (BITVEC
CONS-SERVER-REQUEST SBUS) is set.

*) What the microcode calls "interrupts" are polled for explicitly by
the microcode, and are no special concern of the hardware.

Data manipulation

*) (READ-REG regname)

This refers to the contents of the register <regname>.
<regname> can be an argument register.  This means to use the value of
the place pointed to by the entry at the top of the
corresponding argument stack.

*) (WRITE-REG regname expression)

This means to store into <regname> the value of <expression>.
<regname> can be an argument register.  This means to write into the
place pointed to by the entry at the top of the corresponding argument
stack.

*) (WRITE-SUBFIELD subfield register expression)

This means store into just the subfield <subfield> of <register> the
value of <expression>.

*) (EXTRACT field expression)

This refers to the value of the specified subfield <field> of the
value of <expression>.  The type of the value of <expression> should
be the same as the last element of <field>.

*) (MODIFY field expression subfield-expression)

This refers to the value obtained by starting with the value of
<expression> and changing the subfield <field> of it to contain the
value of <subfield-expression>.  It does not write in any registers;
it simply produces a new value which could be stored with WRITE-REG.

*) (ADD-VALUES exp1 exp2 width)

This refers to a value which is the sum of the values of <exp1> and
<exp2>.  Those two values must be of the same type.  <width> is the
width of that type, supplied for the simulator's sake.

<exp2> can be a constant or a symbol with a constant value.

*) (LEFT-SHIFT-WITH-0-OF expression width)

This refers to the result of shifting the value of <expression> left
one place, bringing in a 0.  <width> is the width of <expression>.

*) (LEFT-SHIFT-WITH-1-OF expression width)

This is like the previous function but shifts in a 1.

*) (RIGHT-SHIFT-WITH-0-OF expression)

This is similar but shifts right.  The width is not needed by the
simulator for this function.

*) (PCLSR-SAVE register)

This causes the contents of <register> to be saved in a special
save-register belonging to <register>.

*) (PCLSR-RESTORE register)

This means to store in <register> the value in its special
save-register.

DEFLOGIC and related things.

*) (DEFLOGIC name args body)

This defines a logic flag named <name>.  It is a function of <args>
computed by <body>.  <body> should be an expression whose value is of
type BIT.  It would usually involve VNOT, VAND, VEQL, etc.  Values of
type BIT can also be obtained by fetching registers or subfields of
type BIT.  =? does NOT produce a value of type BIT; it is a predicate.

*) (logic-flag-name args ...)

This refers to the value that <logic-flag-name> when computed from the
values of the specified args.

*) (VNOT expression)

This refers to the bit-wise complement of the value of <expression>.
It has the same width as <expression>.

VNOT is not allowed just in DEFLOGICs.  You could also ASSIGN
something to the result of a VNOT.

*) VAND, VOR, VXOR

These are like VNOT (but take two args).

*) (VEQL exp1 exp2)

This refers to a BIT value which is 1 if <exp1> and <exp2> are equal,
0 otherwise.  Note the difference between this and =? and (VNOT (VXOR ...)).

*) (VALL field-type exp)

This refers to a value of type BIT which is 1 if all the bits of the
value of <exp> are.  <field-type> should be the type of <exp> so the
simulator can get the width.
